WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:10.457
"The Single Most Important Thing for Anti-Aging Skin Care"

00:00:10.664 --> 00:00:12.480
Considered the single most
important practice

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.040
for maintaining youthful skin:

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.380
the daily application
of sunscreen

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:18.000
and taking
other protective measures,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.080
like wearing a hat.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.420
All the other things
you can do for your skin

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:22.680
pale in comparison,

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.020
especially for those
with pale skin.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.380
Up to 90 percent
of visible facial aging

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.760
among people
with lighter skin tones

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.400
is due to exposure to sunlight.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.100
Those with darker skin
are relatively protected

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.080
due to their built-in
melanin sunscreen,

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.580
but they're still affected.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.820
Sun damage in darker skin
tends to be less about wrinkles

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:43.500
and more
about pigmentation issues,

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.140
such as uneven skin tone,
dark patches,

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.940
and small dark bumps
on the face.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.340
Either way,
dermatologists now agree

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.900
that there is
nothing more important

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.520
to slow the signs of aging

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.100
than to protect your skin
from the sun.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.940
To illustrate, here's
a dramatic photo of a trucker

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.040
who spent decades
getting more sun

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.120
on the left side of his face

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.320
through
his driver's side window,

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.380
reminiscent of a Batman villain.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.800
Factors like sun exposure
and smoking

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.200
can make us look
up to 11 years older.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.600
Compare that to extensive
cosmetic surgery—

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.960
a face lift and a neck lift,
and removing excess skin

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.120
from both the upper
and lower eyelids,

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.220
and a forehead lift,
which combined,

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.320
can make us look
about eight years younger.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.260
So, a healthy lifestyle
may work even better.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.320
Protecting your skin
from the sun

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.060
should be a lifelong endeavor.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.160
This can involve
applying sunscreen,

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:38.280
wearing sun-protective clothing,
hats, and sunglasses,

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.720
and avoiding direct sunlight

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.120
during the peak hours
of 10:00 a.m. to 4:00 p.m.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.520
and instead
seeking shady, covered areas.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:46.740
Sunbathing is frowned upon,

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.540
even with sunscreens
like zinc oxide

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.300
or titanium dioxide that offer
broad-spectrum protection

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.340
against both UVA
and UVB rays.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.860
We now know
that other wavelengths,

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.240
such as near infrared,

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.980
that are not covered
by sunscreens

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.900
also contribute to skin aging.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.360
Men and women
who use tanning beds

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.640
appear significantly older
than those who don't,

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.340
and white women who sunbathe
appear years older

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:09.240
than they actually are,

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.460
similar to what is seen
with smoking.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.780
UVA rays are primarily
responsible for skin aging,

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.360
whereas UVB are the rays
that cause sunburn,

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.900
but a broad-spectrum sunscreen
covering both is recommended

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.140
since both types of UV
contribute to cancer risk.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:26.460
To prevent skin cancer,

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.800
the American Academy
of Dermatology recommends

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.440
sunscreen with an SPF of 30
or higher,

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.940
but an SPF even as low as 15
can help prevent skin aging.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.200
How do we know?

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.340
Because it's been
put to the test.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.560
Despite the widespread belief

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.900
that the use of sunscreen
would prevent skin aging,

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.300
all we had was data
on hairless mice.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:52.020
That is, until ten years ago.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.180
Nine hundred adults
were randomized

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:55.920
either to years
of daily sunscreen use

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.040
or to continue with
their own discretionary use.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.280
(It was considered unethical
to withhold protection

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.500
by giving people
placebo sunscreen.)

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.237
In the end, 77 percent

00:03:06.237 --> 00:03:08.160
in the recommended
daily sunscreen group

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.300
were applying sunscreen

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.220
at least three to four days
per week

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:12.353
compared with only 33 percent

00:03:12.353 --> 00:03:13.980
in the discretionary use group.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:16.080
Would that be
enough of a difference

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.100
to make a difference?

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.940
Yes, there was
significantly less skin aging

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.680
in the instructed
daily use group.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.120
In fact, they suffered

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.920
no detectable increase
in skin aging

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.420
over the four-and-a-half-year
study.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.060
The researchers concluded
"Regular sunscreen use

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.300
retards skin aging in healthy,
middle-aged men and women."

